---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../styles/economics.css';

const title = 'Post-inflation dollars pay pre-inflation debts';
const subhead = 'Inflating fixed debt is real. It is not a clean reset.';
const dek = 'A dollar borrowed before inflation and a dollar paid after inflation are not the same thing. Inflating the real burden of old fixed debt is not the same as resetting the fiscal problem.';

const sources = [
  {
    href: 'https://api.fiscaldata.treasury.gov/services/api/fiscal_service/v2/accounting/od/debt_to_penny?sort=-record_date&page[size]=1',
    also: 'https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/debt-to-the-penny/',
    text: 'U.S. Treasury, Debt to the Penny (TPDO ~$40.09T; debt held by the public ~$32.39T; intragovernmental ~$7.70T as of 17 Sep 2026)',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/static-data/published-reports/mspd-entire/MonthlyStatementPublicDebt_Entire_202608.pdf',
    also: 'https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-statement-public-debt/',
    text: 'U.S. Treasury, Monthly Statement of the Public Debt, 31 Aug 2026 (TIPS ~$2,152,660 million; FRNs ~$679,976 million)',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62105',
    also: 'https://www.cbo.gov/publication/61882',
    text: 'Congressional Budget Office, Budget and Economic Outlook: 2026 to 2036 (debt held by the public ~101% of GDP in 2026 to ~120% in 2036; FY2026 deficit ~$1.9T / ~5.8% of GDP; 2036 deficit ~$3.1T / ~6.7% of GDP; rising net interest as a main driver)',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} — Rycode`}
  description={dek}
  variant="economics"
>
  <article class="econ-digest econ-digest--body">
    <header class="econ-digest-head">
      <h1 class="econ-digest-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="econ-digest-subhead">{subhead}</p>
    </header>

    <p class="econ-digest-lede">A dollar borrowed before inflation and a dollar paid after inflation are not the same thing. That gap is the whole “inflate out of debt” story in one line. When the government (or any borrower) owes a fixed number of dollars, and prices and incomes rise, those later dollars buy less. The real weight of the old debt falls even if the face amount on the bond never changes.</p>
    <p class="econ-digest-lede">That mechanism is real for fixed-coupon, fixed-principal debt. It is also incomplete. Markets can price inflation into new yields. Net interest can climb. Debt held by the public can still rise as a share of the economy. The Congressional Budget Office’s current-law path says that last part out loud. Inflating the real burden of old fixed debt is not the same as resetting the fiscal problem.</p>

    <section class="econ-digest-section" id="two-debt-stocks">
      <h2>Two debt stocks, not one</h2>
      <p>On September 17, 2026, Treasury’s Debt to the Penny series showed total public debt outstanding at about $40.09 trillion ($40,093,343,468,150.50). That headline ~$40 trillion figure is total public debt outstanding (TPDO), the sum of everything the Treasury reports as outstanding.</p>
      <p>The same day splits that stock. Debt held by the public was about $32.39 trillion. Intragovernmental holdings (debt one part of government owes another, such as trust funds) were about $7.70 trillion. Those are different piles. When someone says “the debt is $40 trillion,” ask which stock they mean. Macro analysis of market pressure and interest cost usually tracks debt held by the public. TPDO is the bigger headline. Mixing them is how a round number becomes a wrong claim.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="econ-digest-section" id="fixed-debt">
      <h2>How post-inflation dollars lighten fixed debt</h2>
      <p>Start with a plain bond. You borrow $100 today at a fixed coupon (the interest rate written on the bond) and promise to repay $100 of principal later. If prices double over the life of the loan, and wages and tax receipts rise with them, the $100 you repay is cheaper in real terms (what those dollars can buy) than the $100 you borrowed. The lender is paid in post-inflation dollars. The borrower got pre-inflation dollars. Household purchasing power is the adjustment valve. Taxpayers fund the government in the new price level while old fixed claims stay stuck in the old unit of account.</p>
      <p>State that as a mechanism, not as proof the debt is “paid off.” The face amount is still owed. The real burden can shrink. Those are different sentences.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="econ-digest-section" id="tips-frns">
      <h2>What inflation does not erase the same way</h2>
      <p>Not every Treasury instrument behaves like a fixed nominal bond. Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) adjust principal with the Consumer Price Index (CPI), a common measure of consumer prices. Floating Rate Notes (FRNs) reset their coupon with short-term rates. Inflation and rate resets feed those instruments by design.</p>
      <p>As of the August 31, 2026 Monthly Statement of the Public Debt, TIPS outstanding were about $2.15 trillion and FRNs about $0.68 trillion (statement amounts in millions, TIPS $2,152,660 million and FRNs $679,976 million). That is not the whole marketable stock. It is enough to show that a meaningful slice of the debt does not quietly lose real principal the way a fixed coupon can. “Inflate it away” is a claim about the fixed nominal part of the book, not a wand over every security Treasury issues.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="econ-digest-section" id="not-a-clean-reset">
      <h2>Why it is not a clean reset</h2>
      <p>If inflation alone fixed fiscal stress, you would expect debt held by the public to fall as a share of GDP over time under current policy, or at least stop climbing. CBO’s <cite>Budget and Economic Outlook: 2026 to 2036</cite> projects the opposite under current law. Debt held by the public rises from about 101% of GDP in 2026 to about 120% of GDP in 2036.</p>
      <p>The same outlook puts the fiscal year 2026 deficit near $1.9 trillion, about 5.8% of GDP, growing to about $3.1 trillion, about 6.7% of GDP, by 2036. Rising net interest costs (what the government pays to service its debt after related receipts) are a main driver of those larger deficits. That is the hinge. When markets price inflation into yields, new borrowing and refinancing get more expensive. The real relief on old fixed coupons can arrive alongside a bigger interest bill on the stock that still has to be rolled.</p>
      <p>So pair the mechanism with the path. Pre-inflation dollars versus post-inflation dollars can lighten the real load on fixed nominal coupons. They do not deliver a clean reset when the interest bill and the debt-to-GDP share still climb on the CBO baseline.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="econ-digest-section" id="scoreboard">
      <h2>What the scoreboard actually says</h2>
      <p>Read the day in three facts that stick together.</p>
      <p>First, TPDO near $40 trillion is real on Treasury’s primary series, and debt held by the public near $32 trillion is the market-facing stock. Name which one you mean.</p>
      <p>Second, for fixed-coupon debt, paying with post-inflation dollars reduces real burden. That is arithmetic about units of account, not magic. TIPS and FRNs limit how far that arithmetic travels across the whole book.</p>
      <p>Third, CBO’s current-law outlook still shows debt held by the public rising as a share of GDP, with deficits and net interest headed higher. Inflation priced into yields is how “inflate away” stops being a free lunch.</p>
      <p>If you hear that inflation will take care of the debt, ask two follow-ups. How much of the stock is fixed nominal, and how much is TIPS or floating? And what happens to net interest and debt-to-GDP when markets believe the inflation story? The first question is about mechanism. The second is about whether the mechanism is a reset. On the public numbers above, the answer to the second is no.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="econ-digest-section econ-digest-sources" id="sources">
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ol>
        {sources.map((source) => (
          <li>
            {source.text}: <a href={source.href}>{source.href}</a>
            {source.also && (
              <>
                {' '}and <a href={source.also}>{source.also}</a>
              </>
            )}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <p class="econ-digest-related">Related Economics essays: <a href="/economics/warsh-first-hike-oil-and-five-percent-ten-year/">/economics/warsh-first-hike-oil-and-five-percent-ten-year/</a> · <a href="/economics/still-1998-not-1999/">/economics/still-1998-not-1999/</a> · <a href="/economics/jobs-print-was-strong-mix-is-the-story/">/economics/jobs-print-was-strong-mix-is-the-story/</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Layout>
